From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Attard <samuel.r.attard@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 03:22:21 +0000
Subject: gin: Derive the isolate in gin::Arguments instead of storing it

gin::Arguments stored the isolate in a raw_ptr<v8::Isolate>. Arguments
is constructed and destroyed on the stack for every call to a gin-bound
function, so with BackupRefPtr enabled this was a refcount acquire and
release on the isolate's allocation per call. The isolate is already
reachable from the FunctionCallbackInfo or PropertyCallbackInfo that
the Arguments wraps, so read it from there. This also shrinks Arguments
from 24 to 16 bytes.

Arguments::isolate() returns the same pointer as before for every
constructed Arguments; a default-constructed Arguments still returns
nullptr.

Measured with a gin_unittests micro-benchmark (not part of this CL)
that calls a bound no-op void() 2e6 times from a JS loop, Linux x64
release build with BackupRefPtr enabled: 42.3 -> 27.9 ns per call.

No behaviour change. Adds ArgumentsTest.TestIsolate.

Bug: None

Cherry-picked from
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8455783.
This patch can be removed when that CL lands and rolls into Electron.

